        "findings": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe new unit (`AssessBulkSignup` as its own small function wired between `countNamesakes` and `save`) is the right granularity and matches how this pipeline is built, and persisting the flag on `Owner` follows the existing precedent of storing derived values like `namesakeCount`. But the \"owners created on this business day\" computation is now duplicated.\n\nFindings:\n\n1. **Duplicated daily-count logic** (`AssessBulkSignup.java:23-26` vs `EnsureDailyLimit.java:24-27`). Both steps compute the identical count:\n   ```java\n   ownerRepository.findAll().stream()\n       .filter(existing -> businessDay.equals(existing.getRegistrationDate()))\n       .count();\n   ```\n   Only the threshold differs (80 vs 100). This is exactly the kind of shared derivation the codebase already factors out \u2014 `BusinessDays` exists as the \"single source of truth\" helper so \"every caller \u2026 agrees.\" The owners-created-on-a-business-day count deserves the same treatment: extract a shared helper (e.g. a `countCreatedOn(LocalDate)` on a helper alongside `BusinessDays`, or a repository query method) and have both `EnsureDailyLimit` and `AssessBulkSignup` call it. As written, the two per-day counts can silently drift apart.\n\n2. **Inconsistent source of the business day** (`AssessBulkSignup.java:22`). `EnsureDailyLimit` derives its day via `BusinessDays.effectiveRegistrationDate(request.getRegistrationDate())`, routing through the single-source-of-truth helper. `AssessBulkSignup` instead reads `owner.getRegistrationDate()` directly, relying on `BuildOwner` having already rolled/defaulted the value. That makes the \"effective business day\" concept leak out of `BusinessDays` and become an implicit ordering dependency on `BuildOwner`. If the count logic is extracted per finding #1, route the day derivation through `BusinessDays` consistently (or at minimum make the dependence on build-order explicit) rather than having two steps obtain the same concept two different ways."
      }
